      "textEn": "109\n\nKritant Aasitasamaro Devanam Saha Danavaih. Parshnigraha, Vrito, Viro, Godaityaparajitaah. 13. Meaning of the word:\n\nKruta-antah - destructive war; Asit—was; Samarah—war; Devanam—of the gods; Saha—with; Danavaih—demons;\nParshnigraha—good helper; Vrithah—accepted; Veera—brave; Devaih—by the gods; Daitya—by the demons; Parajitah—who were defeated. In ancient times, a fierce battle took place between the gods and the demons. Having been defeated, the gods\nmade Puranjay their helper, and then they were able to defeat the demons. Therefore, this brave Prajanya\nis called, that is, one who has conquered the abodes of the demons. Vachanadeva Devasya Vishnor Vishwatmanah Prabho.\nVahanatve vrutastasya bhabhubhendro mahavrushah. 14. Meaning of the word\n\nVachanat—from commands or words; Dev-devasya—lord of all gods; Vishnoh—of Lord Vishnu; Vishwa-atmanah—complete; of the universe; Prabhoh—of the Lord or the controller; Vahanatve—because of becoming a vehicle; Vrutah—attached; Tasya—in the service of Puranjay;\nBabhuva—became; Indra—Indra; Maha-vrushah—a large bull. Puranjay agreed to kill all the Asuras on the condition that Indra would become his vehicle. But,\nOut of pride, Indra initially rejected this proposal, but later, by the order of Lord Vishnu,\nHe accepted it, and for the ride of Puranjay, he became a large bull. Having made the bow divinely ready, he shot the arrows. The one who is praised, is approached, and is happy, is situated like a cuckoo. 15. Tejasapyayito Vishnuha Purushasya Mahatmanaha.\nIn the direction of the giants, in the three directions, the city. 16. Meaning of the word:\n\nSah—Puranjay; Sannaddhah—fully engaged; Dhanuh—divine bow; Aadaya—having taken; Vishihan—arrows;\nShitan—extremely sharp; Stuyamanah—extremely praised; Tam—that (bull); Aaruhya—climbing; Yuyutsuha—to fight;\nTaiyasah—with strength; Apyayitah—granted; Vishnoh—of Vishnu; Purushasya—of the supreme being; Maha-atmanah—of the supreme soul; Pratyayam—in the direction; Dishi—in the direction; Daityanam—of the demons; Nyarunat—in possession;\nTaken; Tridashiha—taking the gods along; Puram—to the housing. Having been well protected by the armor and with the desire to fight, Puranjay held his divine bow\nand a very sharp arrow, and being highly praised by the gods, that bull (Indra)"
