पि जंगल के ऋषि राजा पर अत्यन्त दयालु थे, अतएव वे मनोयोगपूर्वक इन्द्रयज्ञ करने लगे",
      "textEn": "112\n\nDhundhumar, thus known, was also Jajvalu. By the fire of the sun, all three remain as residue. 23). Dridhaashwaah, Kapilashwaah, and Bhadraashwaah, thus ends the Bharat. Durdhvashputro Haryashvo Nikumbhasthatsutah smrutah. 24). Meaning of the word\n\nDhundh-marah—one who kills Dhundhu; Iti—in this way; Khyatah—known; Tat-sutah—his sons; Te—all of them; Cha—also;\nJajjuluha—burned; Dhundhoh—of Dhundhu; Mukha-agnina—from the mouth, from the fire that comes out; Sarve—all of them; Trayah—three; Eva—only;\nAvaseshitah—survived; Dridhashva—Dridhashva; Kapilashcha—Kapila; Cha—and; Bhadrashva—Bhadrashva; Iti—Thus; Bharat—O\nKing Parikshit; Dhridhashva-putrah—the son of Dhridhashva; Haryashva—Haryashva; Nikumbha—Nikumbha; Tat-sutah—his son; Smrutah—known..\nOh, King Parikshit, for this reason, Kuvalayashwa is called Dhundhumar. But leaving his three sons,\nthe rest were burnt to ashes by the fire emanating from Dhundhu's mouth. Of the remaining sons,\nare the names—Drudashva, Kapilashva and Bhadrashva.\nDridhashva had a son named Haryashva, whose son is famous by the name Nikumbha. Bahulasva, Nikumbha, Krashasva, and Senajit. Yuvanasva was born, and his descendants went to the forest. 25. Meaning of the word\n\nBahulashva - of Bahulashva; Nikumbhasya - of Nikumbha; Krushashva - Krushashva; Atha - after that; Asya - of Krushashva; Senajit - Senajit;\nYuvanashva - of Yuvanashva; Abhavat - originated; Tasya - of Senajit; Sah - he; Anapatya - without offspring; Vanam Gatah - having become Vanaprasthi\nWent to the forest. Nikumbha's son was Bahulashwa, Bahulashwa's son Krishashwa and Krishashwa's son Senajit. The son of Senjit was Yuwanshv. Yuvanasva had no children, so he renounced worldly life\nand went to the forest. His wife, the learned sages, were his benefactors.\nThose who are pleased with this practice, with their own minds, are well-pleased. 26. Meaning of the word:\n\nBharya-shaten—with a hundred wives; Nirvinnah—extremely sad; Rishayah—the group of sages; Asya—on him; Kripalavah—extremely\nKripalu; Istim—an ritual; Smam—in the past tense; Vartayam Chakrur—started to complete; Aindrim—Indra's sacrifice; Te—they all; Su-\nSamahitah—extremely attentive. Although Yuvanashva went to the forest with his hundred wives, but they were all very sad. However, the sage of the forest was very merciful to the king. Therefore, he began to perform Indrayagya with concentration"
