नाऊँगा कि मैं संसारी राजाओं की कन्याओं का ही नहीं अपितु सुर-सुन्दरियों के लिए भी\nअभीष्ट बन जाऊँ।\n\nमुनिः प्रवेशितः क्षत्रा कन्यान्तःपुरमृद्धिमत् ।",
      "textEn": "117\n\nBeing agile, he rose; to the king (Mandhata), the Brahmin (Saubhari Rishi) came; the daughter, the only daughter;\nHe asked for her; he, the king,\nHe said, \"Take her.\" You can take her; the Brahmin,\nHe desires.\nThe daughter, the daughter; in the Swayamvara, in self-choice, in the Swayamvara. Soubhari Rishi was absorbed in penance in the deep waters of the Yamuna River when he saw a pair of fishes\nin a state of mating. In this way, he experienced the pleasure of worldly life, inspired by which he\nwent to King Mandhata and requested him for a daughter. In response to this plea,\nthe king said, \"Oh Brahmin, any of my daughters can choose anyone as her husband at her own will.\"”\n\nMeaning: Here begins the story of Sauvāri Rīśa. According to Vishwanath Chakraborty Thakur,\nMandhata was the king of Mathura, and Soubhari Rishi was performing penance in the deep waters of the Yamuna River.\nWhen in the sage\nthe desire awoke, he came out of the water and went to King Mandhata and said that he should marry one of his\ndaughters to him. I am the one who is very dear to the women, who is jealous. Valipalita Ejatka Ityaham Pratyudahatruta. 41. I will make myself available to the gods and goddesses. That the Lord is the cause of all causes. 42. Shabdartha\n\nSaha—that, Saubhari Muni said; thinking—by thinking; Apriyam—not pleasing; Striinam—by women; Jarathah—\nDue to old age, weak; Aham—I; Asat-matah—by them unwanted; Vali—with wrinkles; Palitah—with white hair;\nAjat-kah—always shaking the head; Iti—in this way; Aham—I; Pratyudahrutah—left; Sadhayishye—I will do; Tatha—like this;\nManuj-indram—of the princesses of the kings of the world; Iti—in this way; Vyasasitah—Satisfied; Prabhu—Powerful; Yogi—A yogi; Saubhari—A powerful warrior.\nSaubhari Muni thought: I am now weak due to old age. My hair has turned white.\nMy skin is drooping, and my head always keeps shaking. In addition, I am a yogi. Therefore, these\nwomen do not like me. Since the king has rejected me, therefore I will make my body\nsuch that I will not only become the daughter of worldly kings but also for the beautiful women\nI will become desired. The sage entered the inner chambers of the Kshatriya's maiden."
